+## Subtopics
+
+### [Off-Axis Positional Encoding](3503-off-axis-positional-encoding.md)
+Off-Axis Positional Encoding is a feature representation technique for neural radiance fields that encodes the full covariance structure of anisotropic Gaussians, rather than just their marginal variances along axis-aligned directions [3503-off-axis-positional-encoding.md]. It solves the ambiguity where axis-aligned encodings produce identical features for distinct Gaussian shapes that share the same diagonal covariance elements. It differs from standard Fourier Positional Encoding by utilizing a non-identity basis matrix derived from icosahedron vertices to capture off-axis correlations, enabling accurate Kalman-like warping in the presence of highly anisotropic primitives.
+
+### [Integrated Positional Encoding](5255-integrated-positional-encoding.md)
+Integrated Positional Encoding (IPE) is a feature representation that generalizes standard positional encoding to allow a region of space to be compactly featurized, rather than encoding a single point. It solves the aliasing artifacts that occur in neural radiance fields when scene content is observed at multiple resolutions by integrating sine and cosine features over an interval (cone) instead of evaluating them at a single point. This effectively anti-aliases high-frequency features by shrinking them towards zero when the frequency period is small compared to the interval size.
+
+### Scale Featurization
+Scale Featurization is a method to provide scale information to the MLP by averaging and concatenating a featurized version of the downweighting factors $\omega_{j,\ell}$, ensuring features have consistent magnitude regardless of training progression [S14]. The featurized scale input is calculated as $(2 \cdot \operatorname{mean}_j(\downweight_{j, \ell}) - 1)\sqrt{\initval^2 + \stopgrad(\operatorname{mean}(\grid_\ell^2))}$, where $\downweight_{j, \ell}$ is the downweighting factor, $\initval$ is the initialization magnitude of $V_\ell$, and $\grid_\ell$ represents the values in $V_\ell$ [S14]. A stop-gradient operator is applied to the mean of the squared grid values to prevent optimization from indirectly modifying the scale-feature via $V_\ell$ [S14]. For speed, an approximation for erf(x) is used: $\operatorname{erf}(x) \approx \operatorname{sign}(x) \sqrt{1 - \exp(-(\frac{4}{\pi})x^2)}$ [S14]. This has no discernible impact on quality and only a marginal impact on performance [S14].
+
+### Fourier Feature Networks
+Fourier Feature Networks is a technique that addresses the problem of neural networks struggling to model complex, high-frequency signals from low-frequency inputs by introducing Fourier features as a preprocessing step to encode low-frequency inputs, thereby introducing higher-frequency information [S15, unofficial]. Fourier features replace the featurizer or kernel that the neural net would otherwise need to learn, allowing the network to predict signals (1D, 2D, or 3D radiance fields) more effectively than standard MLPs which suffer from spectral bias [S15, unofficial]. It is used to predict 1-D signals from time, image pixel values from position, and color/opacity from 3D position and view direction to model radiance fields for neural avatars and view-dependent effects [S15, unofficial].
+
+### Coordinate-Time Encoding
+Coordinate-Time Encoding is an encoding scheme that concatenates interpolated features with positionally encoded coordinates and temporal embeddings to enhance temporal information and handle dynamic light sources in in vivo sequences, replacing standard positional encoding of viewing directions [S16]. The formula is $\mathbf{b}(s) = g_{s, \xi^2}\left(\frac{i - 0.5}{k}\right), i = 1, 2, 3 \dots k$, where $g_{s,\xi^2}$ is a Gaussian kernel with mean value $s$ and variance $\xi^2$, mapping $s \in \mathbb{R}$ into a higher dimensional space $\mathbb{R}^k$ [S16]. One-blob encoding is used separately on each of the four coordinate values [S16]. $k$ is the dimensionality of the encoded space [S16].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** $O(N \cdot D \cdot F)$, where $N$ is the batch size, $D$ is the input dimension, and $F$ is the number of frequency bands [G]. The operation is element-wise and highly parallelizable on GPUs [G].
-- **Memory Complexity:** $O(N \cdot D \cdot F)$ for the output tensor [G]. The frequency bands are pre-computed and stored once [G].
-- **Measured Performance:** In NeRF, using 10 frequency bands for position and 4 for view directions allows the MLP to reconstruct high-frequency details like text and fine geometry that a raw MLP cannot [S2, S4, unofficial].
-- **Strengths:** Simple to implement, differentiable, and effective at overcoming spectral bias in MLPs [G].
-- **Limitations:** The frequencies are fixed and not learned [G]. If the scene contains frequencies outside the range of the chosen bands, details will be lost [G]. The output dimension grows linearly with the number of bands, which can increase the size of the MLP's input layer [G].
-- **When to Use:** When training an MLP to represent a continuous signal (like a neural radiance field) that contains high-frequency details [G].
-- **When Not to Use:** When the signal is very smooth and low-frequency, or when memory constraints prevent the increased input dimensionality [G].
+**Time and Memory Complexity:**
+- Time: $O(B \cdot D \cdot L)$, where $B$ is batch size, $D$ is input dimension, and $L$ is number of octaves [S4, unofficial].
+- Memory: $O(B \cdot D \cdot L)$ for the output tensor [S4, unofficial].
+
+**Measured Performance:**
+- In NeRF, $L=10$ for position and $L=4$ for direction are standard [S6, S9, unofficial].
+- Output dimension for position: 63 features [S6, S9, unofficial].
+- Output dimension for direction: 27 features [S6, S9, unofficial].
+
+**Strengths and Limitations:**
+- **Strengths:** Effectively mitigates spectral bias, allowing MLPs to learn high-frequency details [S8, S15, unofficial]. Simple to implement and differentiable [S1, S3, unofficial].
+- **Limitations:** Fixed frequencies may not adapt to the specific frequency content of the scene. High frequencies can lead to aliasing if not integrated (solved by [Integrated Positional Encoding](5255-integrated-positional-encoding.md)) [S1, unofficial].
+
+**When to Use:**
+- Use when training an MLP-based neural field (like NeRF or SIREN) to represent continuous functions [S1, S6, S15, unofficial].
+- Use when the input coordinates need to capture high-frequency variations [S4, S9, unofficial].
+
+**When Not to Use:**
+- Use [Integrated Positional Encoding](5255-integrated-positional-encoding.md) if aliasing artifacts are observed due to multi-resolution rendering [S1, unofficial].
+- Use [Off-Axis Positional Encoding](3503-off-axis-positional-encoding.md) if dealing with highly anisotropic Gaussian primitives [3503-off-axis-positional-encoding.md].
 
 ## Background
-The method is a core component of NeRF pipelines and SIREN-based architectures [S1, S2, S4, unofficial]. It is inspired by the use of sinusoidal positional encodings in Transformer architectures, but adapted for continuous coordinate representation [G]. It helps the network learn high-frequency scene details [S1, S4, unofficial].
+Fourier Positional Encoding was introduced in the context of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in "NeRF: Representing Scenes as Neural Radiance Fields for View Synthesis" (ECCV 2020) [S6, unofficial]. It was used to help the MLP in NeRF learn high-frequency details of the scene, which raw coordinates could not represent effectively due to the spectral bias of MLPs [S6, S11, unofficial]. The method maps 3D positions and viewing directions to higher-dimensional spaces using sinusoidal functions at exponentially increasing frequencies [S1, S3, S6, S9, unofficial].
 
